<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · disaster</p><h1 class="enc-title">Western African Ebola epidemic</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2013">2013</a>–<a href="/years/2016">2016</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a> · Also known as West African Ebola epidemic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Western African Ebola epidemic was the largest outbreak of Ebola virus disease on record, spreading from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> in late 2013 to Liberia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>. It caused more than 28,000 cases and over 11,000 deaths, nearly 5,000 of them in Liberia, closing schools and halting much economic life before it was brought under control in 2016.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/western-african-ebola-epidemic.jpg" alt="Western African Ebola epidemic" width="320" height="171" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ZeLonewolf · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2014_ebola_virus_epidemic_in_West_Africa_simplified.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The epidemic was caused by the Ebola virus, one of four disease-causing viruses in the genus Ebolavirus. Since its discovery in 1976, Ebola virus disease had been confined to Middle Africa, where it is native. Studies have shown that the outbreak was likely caused by a lineage that spread from Middle Africa through an animal host within the preceding decade, with the first transfer to humans in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a>. Bats are strongly implicated as reservoir hosts, and the index case is suspected to have followed a child's contact with an insectivorous bat from a colony near the village of Meliandou.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The first cases were recorded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> in December 2013. On 25 March 2014 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-health-organization">World Health Organization</a> reported an outbreak in four southeastern districts of Guinea, with 86 suspected cases including 59 deaths. Thinking the spread contained, Médecins Sans Frontières closed its treatment centres in May, but large numbers of new cases reappeared in late August. The disease spread to Liberia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>, with minor outbreaks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, and isolated cases in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>, the United Kingdom and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Secondary infections of medical workers occurred in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><p>Extreme poverty, dysfunctional health systems, distrust of government after years of armed conflict, a delay of several months in responding, burial customs of washing the body and spread into densely populated cities all contributed to the failure to control it. A public health emergency of international concern was declared on 8 August 2014, and a cordon sanitaire was set up that day around the area where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a>, Liberia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a> meet and where 70 per cent of known cases had been found. Cases peaked in October 2014 and then declined after substantial international resources were committed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>By the end, 28,616 people had been infected and 11,310 had died, a case fatality rate of 40 per cent, although the WHO believes the figures understate the outbreak. It ended its emergency status on 29 March 2016, and the epidemic was declared over on 9 June 2016, 42 days after the last case tested negative.</p><p>About 17,000 people survived, many with post-Ebola syndrome, symptoms severe enough to need care for months or years. Later the WHO announced that a two-year trial of the rVSV-ZEBOV vaccine appeared to protect against the strain responsible. The WHO called the aftermath an emergency within an emergency for families, health systems, economies and social structures.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Western African Ebola epidemic” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Reconstruction under Johnson Sirleaf from 2006 brought debt relief and renewed investment, but the <b>Ebola epidemic</b> of 2014–2015 killed several thousand Liberians and strained a weak health system.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liberia">Liberia · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
